import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import schema from '../../data/schema.json'
---

<Base title="Privacy, consent and security" description="The rules the core enforces for every adapter, and how they are tested.">
  <h1>Privacy, consent and security</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    These plugins get installed on checkout pages, so they have to be safer by default than hand-written
    tracking code. Every rule below lives in the shared core, not in individual adapters, and the conformance
    suite fails a release that breaks one.
  </p>

  <h2>No payment data, ever</h2>
  <p>
    Adapters read the platform's event payloads, never DOM inputs. Card numbers, CVV, expiry and billing
    addresses are stripped in the core <strong>even when a platform payload includes them</strong> — the
    fixtures deliberately carry a <code>card_number</code> so that stripping is exercised rather than assumed.
  </p>

  <h2>PII is off by default</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>usr.id</code> is the platform or provider customer ID. That is the only identifier sent by default.</li>
    <li>Email, name, phone and address need an explicit opt-in (<code>includeEmail</code>, <code>includeName</code>). Phone and address have no opt-in at all.</li>
    <li>Search queries are sent as <code>search_term_length</code> only. The text never leaves the browser, because a shopper can type anything into a search box.</li>
    <li>Free-text fields — notes, comments, messages — are dropped wherever they appear in a payload.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Consent</h2>
  <p>
    When a consent adapter is present it runs first and sets <code>trackingConsent = 'not-granted'</code>
    before the SDK starts, which stops RUM collecting anything, not just this plugin's actions. Commerce
    adapters additionally hold their own events, including <code>setUser</code>, until consent is granted.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Nothing is queued.</strong> Events that happen while the decision is pending are dropped, not
    buffered, so a later grant never backfills pre-consent activity. One consequence worth stating plainly:
    a declined visitor sends nothing at all, so RUM cannot report opt-in rate. A consent plugin describes
    only the categories of sessions that opted in.
  </p>

  <h2>Size limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>items[]</code> is opt-in and capped at {schema.limits.maxItems} entries.</li>
    <li>Strings are truncated at {schema.limits.maxStringLength} characters.</li>
    <li>Nested objects deeper than four levels are dropped rather than partly sent.</li>
    <li><code>commerce.purchase</code> is deduplicated per <code>transaction_id</code> for {schema.limits.dedupeTtlHours} hours in <code>localStorage</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Supply chain</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Packages publish to npm from CI only, with provenance. No local publishes.</li>
    <li>CDN URLs are exact-version jsDelivr links with an SRI hash. There is no auto-updating <code>latest</code> URL; merchants pin a version.</li>
    <li>GitHub Actions are pinned by commit SHA, and workflows run with <code>persist-credentials: false</code>.</li>
    <li>A new runtime dependency on an adapter needs core-maintainer review. The launch adapters have none beyond the shared core.</li>
    <li>Every release runs the conformance suite and the real-SDK integration test.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Declared footprint</h2>
  <p>
    Each adapter declares the platform globals it reads, the events it subscribes to, the storage keys it
    writes and the RUM APIs it calls. The gallery renders that as a permissions screen, debug mode prints it
    to the console, and the conformance suite compares it against what the adapter actually did during its
    fixture run — so an adapter cannot quietly call an API it did not declare.
  </p>

  <h2>What the tests actually check</h2>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Checked by</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Action names are in the schema, with the right family prefix</td><td>Conformance suite, every adapter</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Attribute names and types match the schema</td><td>Conformance suite</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No blocked key is ever sent, on actions or on <code>setUser</code></td><td>Conformance suite plus core unit tests</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Email and name appear only when opted in</td><td>Conformance suite</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>items[]</code> capped, strings truncated</td><td>Conformance suite plus core unit tests</td></tr>
        <tr><td>One purchase per <code>transaction_id</code>, across a reload</td><td>Conformance suite plus a real-browser test</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Nothing leaves the browser before consent</td><td>A real-browser test with the intake intercepted</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The declared footprint matches the run</td><td>Conformance suite plus the manifest check</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</Base>
